#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105438 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105718 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bad93a16-17d4-50ff-89c8-f3d966df5f1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unknown Quantities in Quadrilaterals and Pentagons"}] :question/id #uuid "5f89d47f-4068-508e-9fdd-1f3768d53a92" :question/math-academy-id "e-10637" :question/problem "Given the pentagon $ABCDE$ shown below, find the value of $x$.\n\n![](images/dc/dc4407245e0639d9055d9518088a46b310f67e897337bd8963e5b1c42a86421f.png)" :question/worked-solution "A pentagon has $n=5$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon must be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180^{∘} \\cdot (n - 2) &= 180^{∘} \\cdot (5 - 2) \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 540^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the internal angles and solving for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠B + m∠C + m∠D + m∠E &= 540^{∘} \\\\\n120^{∘} + 110^{∘} + 2x + 90^{∘} + x + 40^{∘} &= 540^{∘} \\\\\n2x + x + 40^{∘} + 120^{∘} + 110^{∘} + 90^{∘} &= 540^{∘} \\\\\n3x + 360^{∘} &= 540^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 60^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105439 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105719 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b111120-41dd-5a58-bcc5-b0b6fc78b384" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Sides of a Polygon Given the Sum of Its Interior Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "7d0dd769-07d8-58c6-a2c8-d6165ca5bb5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10638" :question/problem "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon is $720^\\circ$. How many sides does it have?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides is $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2)$.\n\nTo find the number of sides in our polygon, we start by setting up the following equation:\n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2) = 720^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $n$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180^{∘} \\cdot (n - 2) &= 720^{∘} \\\\\nn - 2 &= \\frac{720^{∘}}{180^{∘}} \\\\\nn - 2 &= 4 \\\\\nn &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polygon has $n=6$ sides (it is a hexagon)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105440 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105717 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d6d5827-91a8-5fd4-85ea-7564e8f5396e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Interior Angles of a Quadrilateral"}] :question/id #uuid "757aacb7-c988-5f1f-9ae6-c69ebf1399ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-10772" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the interior angles of a quadrilateral?\n\n1. $40^{\\circ}$, $50^{\\circ}$, $100^{\\circ}$, $170^{\\circ}$\n2. $20^{\\circ}$, $50^{\\circ}$, $150^{\\circ}$, $140^{\\circ}$\n3. $30^{\\circ}$, $35^{\\circ}$, $140^{\\circ}$, $175^{\\circ}$" :question/worked-solution "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral must be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n180^{∘} \\cdot (n - 2) &= 180^{∘} \\cdot (4 - 2) \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 360^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check this for the above three options:\n\n$$\n40^{∘} + 50^{∘} + 100^{∘} + 170^{∘} = 360^{∘}✓; 20^{∘} + 50^{∘} + 150^{∘} + 140^{∘} = 360^{∘}✓; 30^{∘} + 35^{∘} + 140^{∘} + 175^{∘} = 380^{∘}X\n$$\n\nTherefore, only options I and II could be the angles of a quadrilateral."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105717 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d6d5827-91a8-5fd4-85ea-7564e8f5396e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Interior Angles of a Quadrilateral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8464769e-d101-5cc6-84af-8419f4445e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104957} {:answer/id #uuid "874896de-afd0-5d26-86be-c0da5f57ef72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104958} {:answer/id #uuid "9c49e928-c7db-5b84-bae0-a98681af5056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104959} {:answer/id #uuid "07de0afd-378b-51c4-b9ed-bed37e8b7764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104960} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca84bc5-9fd1-5197-b13f-00e365f7abb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104961}] :answer-field/id #uuid "20ef2487-e6d2-501a-8f40-6e3e3c076726" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104956}] :question/id #uuid "402ba7bd-dca2-507f-827e-423295d57d86" :question/math-academy-id "q-68022" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the interior angles of a quadrilateral?\n\n1. $64^{∘}$, $73^{∘}$, $98^{∘}$, $124^{∘}$\n2. $73^{∘}$, $112^{∘}$, $93^{∘}$, $82^{∘}$\n3. $80^{∘}$, $140^{∘}$, $49^{∘}$, $84^{∘}$"}]}
